.\" See the note at the top of proxmodctl.8 about why this is hand-written roff.
.TH PROXMOD-VERIFY 8 "2026-08-27" "proxmod 0.2.1" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
proxmod-verify \- check that proxmod is loaded and working on this host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B proxmod-verify
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-quiet ]
.RB [ \-\-live-only ]
.RB [ \-\-registry-only ]
.RB [ \-\-list ]
.RB [ \-\-no-http ]
.RB [ \-\-url
.IR URL ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B proxmod-verify
answers one question: is proxmod actually doing what it claims to be doing on
this host, right now. It is what
.B "proxmodctl status"
runs, what
.B proxmod-verify.service
runs at boot, and what
.B proxmod-reapply
consults before it decides whether to restart anything.
.PP
The distinction it exists for is between
.I registered
and
.IR reachable .
An extension can install its API methods without complaint and still be
unreachable, because a Proxmox upgrade moved the path it mounted under. Nothing
logs that. This command replays the routes proxmod registered and resolves each
one, so the failure has a name before an administrator finds it by hand.
.PP
Run without arguments it checks everything and prints a report. Every check has
an id, so a monitoring system can alert on one finding rather than on the exit
status alone.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable report on stdout.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
No output; the exit status is the answer.
.TP
.B \-\-live-only
Check only whether the running daemons have proxmod loaded at all. This is the
narrow question
.B proxmod-reapply
asks before it restarts anything, and it is deliberately narrow: a failing HTTP
check is not a reason to bounce
.BR pvedaemon .
.TP
.B \-\-registry-only
Check only whether the running daemons loaded the extension registry that is on
disk now, and print that registry's fingerprint on stdout. Exit
.B 0
up to date,
.B 1
out of date,
.B 2
could not tell.
.TP
.B \-\-list
List the extensions installed on this host and the state of each \-
.BR effective ", " disabled ", " masked " or " unresolved .
Read off the disk rather than out of a running daemon, so it still answers when
the daemons are down, which is when it is asked. Checks nothing; exits
.B 0
unless the registry itself could not be read. This is
.BR "proxmodctl list" .
.TP
.B \-\-no-http
Skip the checks that talk to the live web interface.
.TP
.BI \-\-url " URL"
Base URL of the local
.BR pveproxy .
Default
.IR https://127.0.0.1:8006 .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Healthy.
.TP
.B 1
A check failed. Under
.B \-\-registry-only
this means the loaded registry is stale, which is a convergence job, not a
fault.
.TP
.B 2
.B \-\-registry-only
could not tell.
.TP
.B 64
Usage error.
.SH NOTES
The structural check runs in a fresh
.BR perl ,
not inside
.BR pvedaemon ,
so an extension that this process cannot load is reported as a warning rather
than an error: the daemons have a different
.B @INC
and may well have loaded it. The live check, which asks the running daemons, is
the authority on that question.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR proxmodctl (8),
.BR pvedaemon (8),
.BR pveproxy (8)
.PP
.IR /usr/share/doc/proxmod/verification.md ,
which lists every check id and what each one means.
.SH BUGS
https://github.com/CrunchyMonkies/proxmod/issues
